// Conformance row W19 — the BITWISE operators `& | ^` take rule 1 (design 195;
// a row the matrix did not carry, found by unit 1's probes)
//
// `&`, `|` and `^` are two-peer operations: the result is a mask over both
// operands, so both operands describe the same bit positions and therefore the
// same width. Only the SHIFTS keep row 6's exemption, because a shift's right
// operand is a count rather than a peer.
//
// PROBE (Aug 10, before the fix): this COMPILED. The lowering brought the right
// operand to the left's width with a ZERO extension whatever its signedness, so
// `a & b` for a negative `Int16 b` masked against `0x000000000000FFFF` instead
// of an all-ones word — a silently wrong mask, and the only rule-1 position
// whose old behavior was neither a crash nor a right answer.
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: requires both operands to have the same type
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: `Int16`

func mask(a: Int, b: Int16) -> Int {
    a & b
}

func main() {
    print(mask(6, -1i16))
}
